{"name":"Blue Shale","hex":"#b9cacc","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"shale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A shell, scale or husk; a cod or pod.","A fine-grained sedimentary rock of a thin, laminated, and often friable, structure."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To take off the shell or coat of."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shale"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shale","blocks":[{"text":"Shale is a fine-grained, clastic sedimentary rock formed from mud that is a mix of flakes of clay minerals (hydrous aluminium phyllosilicates, e.g., kaolin, Al2Si2O5(OH)4) and tiny fragments (silt-sized particles) of other minerals, especially quartz and calcite. Shale is characterized by its tendency to split into thin layers (laminae) less than one centimeter in thickness. This property is called fissility. Shale is the most common sedimentary rock."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Shale_formation%2C_Marble_Canyon%2C_Kootenay_National_Park%2C_British_Columbia%2C_2025-07-11.jpg/330px-Shale_formation%2C_Marble_Canyon%2C_Kootenay_National_Park%2C_British_Columbia%2C_2025-07-11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shale"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg/500px-Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg/1280px-Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg/500px-Drill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sample of drill cuttings of shale while drilling an oil well in Louisiana, United States. Sand grain = 2 mm in diameter","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADrill_cuttings_-_Annotated_-_2004.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Mudrock_Colors.jpg/500px-Mudrock_Colors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Mudrock_Colors.jpg/1280px-Mudrock_Colors.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Mudrock_Colors.jpg/500px-Mudrock_Colors.jpg?utm_source=en.